Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the "eventual" bound $L(x) \leq x^\delta$ (for large $x$) extends to a "global" bound $L(x) \leq C\,x^\delta$ (for all $x \geq 1$).

## Analysis

The key observation is that slow variation only constrains the behavior of $L$ **at infinity**. The definition requires $\lim_{x\to\infty} L(tx)/L(x) = 1$ for all $t > 0$, which depends solely on the values of $L$ for large arguments. The values of $L$ on any compact subset of $(0,\infty)$ are completely unconstrained by the slow variation property.

The "eventual" bound $L(x) \leq x^\delta$ for $x \geq x_0(\delta)$ controls $L$ on $[x_0, \infty)$. To extend this to a global bound on $[1, \infty)$, we would need $L(x)/x^\delta$ to be bounded on $[1, x_0]$. Since $x^\delta$ is bounded above and below by positive constants on $[1, x_0]$, this is equivalent to requiring $L$ to be bounded on $[1, x_0]$. But slow variation alone does **not** guarantee local boundedness.

## Counterexample

Define $L:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ by
$$
L(x) = \begin{cases} \dfrac{1}{|x - \tfrac{3}{2}|} & \text{if } x \in [1,2]\setminus\{\tfrac{3}{2}\}, \\[6pt] 1 & \text{if } x = \tfrac{3}{2} \text{ or } x \notin [1,2]. \end{cases}
$$

**$L$ is slowly varying at infinity.** For any $t > 0$, take $X = 2/t$. For all $x > X$, both $x > 2$ and $tx > 2$, so $L(x) = 1$ and $L(tx) = 1$, giving $L(tx)/L(x) = 1$. Hence $\lim_{x\to\infty} L(tx)/L(x) = 1$ for every $t > 0$.

**The eventual bound holds.** For any $\delta > 0$ and all $x > 2$, we have $L(x) = 1 \leq x^\delta$ (since $x > 1$).

**No global bound exists.** Fix any $\delta > 0$ and any $C > 0$. Consider $x$ approaching $\frac{3}{2}$ from within $[1,2]$. Then
$$
\frac{L(x)}{x^\delta} = \frac{1}{|x - \tfrac{3}{2}|\cdot x^\delta} \to +\infty \quad \text{as } x \to \tfrac{3}{2},
$$
since $x^\delta \to (3/2)^\delta > 0$ while $|x - 3/2| \to 0$. Therefore, for $x$ sufficiently close to $\frac{3}{2}$, we have $L(x) > C\,x^\delta$.

Since $C$ was arbitrary, no positive constant $C$ (depending on $\delta$ or otherwise) can satisfy $L(x) \leq C\,x^\delta$ for all $x \geq 1$.

## Conclusion

The answer is **no**. The slow variation property controls $L$ only at infinity, leaving its behavior on compact subsets of $(0,\infty)$ entirely free. A slowly varying function can have singularities (or be unbounded) on compact intervals, which prevents the eventual bound $L(x) \leq x^\delta$ from being extended to a global bound $L(x) \leq C\,x^\delta$ on all of $[1,\infty)$.

The implication **would** hold under the additional assumption that $L$ is locally bounded on $(0,\infty)$ (i.e., bounded on every compact subinterval), since then $\sup_{x \in [1, x_0]} L(x)/x^\delta < \infty$ and one could take $C = \max\!\bigl(1,\, \sup_{x \in [1,x_0]} L(x)/x^\delta\bigr)$. But local boundedness is not a consequence of slow variation alone.

